 Richter & Darabi 2023, p. 168 "Considering the prosperous sedentary life, building tradition and imagery-based symbolic trends and cultural influence since the Younger Dryas, it is apparent that Körtiktepe (and likely other unexcavated Late Epipalaeolithic sites in the region) was an important predecessor of the PPNA culture in the Upper Tigris Basin, which later flourished with Hallan Çemi, Çayönü, Demirköy Höyük, Hasankeyf Höyük, Çemka Höyük, Gusir Höyük and Boncuklu Tarla. It is also likely that, if not the predecessor, Körtiktepe at least had a great influence on the animal symbolism at the dawn of the Neolithic, with a lasting effect on subsequent PPN sites such as Göbeklitepe, Karahantepe, Jerf el Ahmar, Dja'de el Mughara and Nevalı Çori in the Middle Euphrates Basin, as well as Nemrik 9 and Qermez Dere in the Middle Tigris Basin."
